# IAX-8b — a registrar that skips the challenge and omits REFRESH.
#
# Two RFC behaviours in one datagram:
#
#  1. No REGAUTH at all. "The IAX protocol does permit servers to forego the
#     challenge process described above. This open approach is inherently
#     insecure and does nothing to prevent unauthorized usage." (§10) A
#     registrant must still cope with a REGACK answering its first REGREQ.
#  2. No REFRESH IE. "If no 'refresh' is sent, a default registration
#     expiration of 60 seconds MUST be assumed by both peers." (§6.1.1,
#     restated §6.1.4 and §8.6.18)

# REGACK — subclass 0x0f (§8.4).
#   80 42       source call number 0x0042 (the registrar's)
#   00 01       destination call number 1 (ours)
#   00000032    time-stamp 50 ms
#   00 01       OSeqno 0, ISeqno 1 — it has seen our first REGREQ (OSeqno 0)
# Payload:
#   06 06 "n0call"  USERNAME — MUST be sent with REGACK        §8.6.6
#   12 10 …         APPARENT ADDR — "MUST indicate the 'apparent address'"
#                   (§6.1.1). Family bytes 02 00 again: the RFC's own
#                   self-contradictory example (trap 13).       §8.6.17
# No DATETIME (SHOULD, not MUST) and no REFRESH.
8042 0001 00000032 00 01 06 0f  06 06 6e30 6361 6c6c  12 10 0200 11d9 cb007109 0000000000000000
